            <ArticleTitle>Brucellar spondylodiscitis: noncontiguous multifocal involvement of the cervical, thoracic, and lumbar spine.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>214-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Brucellosis is a zoonosis of worldwide distribution presenting with a wide clinical spectrum. Brucellosis can involve any organ or system. The axial skeleton is the most common site of involvement with a frequency ranging from 2% to 53%. Multiple-level spinal involvements are rare. This report describes the first case of noncontiguous synchronous multifocal involvement of all cervical, thoracic, and lumbar regions in a patient with brucellar spondylodiscitis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
